Section: The Bound Card

 Cells 36264 through 36319 of the low-memory band documented in
 ArchitectsSketchbook. The seam takes no device argument and there is
 nowhere else to keep one: a module-level record binding is a recipe
 rather than a cell, and referencing it allocates the record again on
 every reference, which on a path polled tens of millions of times per
 accept is the leak the control block exists to avoid.

 Every value here fits in 32 bits. The heap arena ends at 3 GB and a
 device BAR is refused unless it lands between 3 and 4 GB, so no address
 the seam stores can exceed the low word.

  net-driver-cb : Integer = 36264

  net-driver-off-card : Integer = 0
  net-driver-off-mmio : Integer = 8
  net-driver-off-rx-ring : Integer = 16
  net-driver-off-rx-bufs : Integer = 24
  net-driver-off-ctrl-blk : Integer = 32
  net-driver-off-tx-ring : Integer = 40
  net-driver-off-tx-bufs : Integer = 48

  net-card-ne2k : Integer = 0
  net-card-e1000 : Integer = 1

  net-driver-cell : Integer -> Integer
  net-driver-cell (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (net-driver-cb + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  net-driver-active : Integer
  net-driver-active = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-card

Section: Bind

  net-driver-bind-e1000 : E1000Device -> Integer
  net-driver-bind-e1000 (d) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then net-card-ne2k
   else if (d.e-mac-valid) == False then net-card-ne2k
   else let w1 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-mmio) 0 (d.e-mmio)
   in let w2 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-rx-ring) 0 (d.e-rx-ring)
   in let w3 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-rx-bufs) 0 (d.e-rx-bufs)
   in let w4 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-ctrl-blk) 0 (d.e-ctrl-blk)
   in let w5 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-tx-ring) 0 (d.e-tx-ring)
   in let w6 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-tx-bufs) 0 (d.e-tx-bufs)
   in let sel = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-card) 0 net-card-e1000
   in net-card-e1000

Section: Bring-up

 The SHAPE here is card-independent and the finder behind it is not. If
 the sitting reports a part that is not Intel, `e1000-find` and
 `e1000-init` are what change and this function does not. That is the
 reason it holds no device knowledge of its own: no device-ID table, no
 quirk, no register.

  net-driver-bring-up : Integer
  net-driver-bring-up =
   when e1000-find pci-scan-all
     is Just (d) -> net-driver-bind-e1000 (e1000-init d)
     is None -> net-card-ne2k

  net-driver-card-name : Integer -> Text
  net-driver-card-name (c) = if c == net-card-e1000 then "e1000" else "ne2000"

Section: Station Address

 It is read live off RAL/RAH rather than cached in the control block. A
 cached copy would be one more thing that can disagree with the hardware,
 and the two register reads cost less than the six-byte list they answer.

  net-driver-ne2k-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]

  net-driver-mac : List Integer
  net-driver-mac =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then e1000-read-mac (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio)
   else net-driver-ne2k-mac

Section: Send

 e1000-send-at reads four addresses off an E1000Device and never reads
 the station address, so the record rebuilt here carries an empty MAC.
 It is rebuilt per FRAME SENT rather than per poll, which is the reason
 this side may spend an allocation the receive side may not.

  net-driver-e1000-device : E1000Device
  net-driver-e1000-device =
   E1000Device {
     e-mmio = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio,
     e-rx-ring = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-ring,
     e-tx-ring = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-tx-ring,
     e-rx-bufs = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-bufs,
     e-tx-bufs = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-tx-bufs,
     e-ctrl-blk = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-ctrl-blk,
     e-present = True,
     e-mac = [],
     e-mac-valid = False
   }

  net-driver-send-frame : List Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  net-driver-send-frame (frame) =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then e1000-send-frame net-driver-e1000-device frame
   else ne2k-send-frame frame

Section: Receive

 The receive path is polled up to fifty million times per accept, so it
 takes the raw entry point and builds no device record. The
 __heap-save / __heap-restore pair that makes those polls survivable
 stays in the callers, wrapped around this call.

  net-driver-recv-frame : [Network.Read] List Integer
  net-driver-recv-frame =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then (e1000-poll-raw
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-ring)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-bufs)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-ctrl-blk)).r-frame
   else ne2k-recv-frame

